ve creates a continuing obligation that complicates a shared household move.\",\n      \"accept\": true\n    }\n  ],\n  \"bindings\": [\n    {\n      \"key\": \"S223\",\n      \"assessment\": \"The supplied card fingerprint and seed IDs still identify the same memory-reinterpretation premise. The preserved a hash matches; the transfer's b hash is stale relative to the supplied candidateHash. The picnic-photograph action, changed-smile opening, other-seat response, unchanged-photograph challenge and second-meaning closing remain thematically compatible, though they do not independently ensure comfort, evidence and accusation. The replacement painting seed preserves the first painting, hall display and early belief used by S223+D226-b. S223+D226-a still uses the preserved window-seat seed and D226's overseas-job/shop choice; the supplied D226-b spare-key seed supplies consequential choice structure rather than the painting object. Rebinding does not disrupt those uses. The shared seedUse description's possible accusation is not realized by the b pair's appreciative sacrifice ending; that existing limitation remains and is not repaired or newly scored here. Draft status, provenance, versions, source hashes and reviewed fingerprints are unchanged.\",\n      \"compatible\": true\n    },\n    {\n      \"key\": \"S224\",\n      \"assessment\": \"The fingerprint and both seed IDs retain the same favor-accounting meaning. The preserved a binding hash matches, while the b binding hash is stale. Reversing IOU names in the action and changing debt ownership in the response fit the replacement's actual creditor reversal. The opening, repayment challenge and insufficient-original-favor closing emphasize A becoming indebted again, as in the preserved bike/dinner alternative; they remain compatible with the replacement's broader recalculation behavior without reproducing its final direction. There are no bespoke pairs or supporting seeds to rebind. The compiled-pair-draft status do